        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most homes, the best generator cord is a 25-foot, 10-gauge L14-30 cord running from the generator to an inlet box or transfer switch. Large generators with a 14-50 outlet need a 6-gauge, 50-amp cord, inverters with an RV outlet need a TT-30 cord, and households without a transfer switch need a splitter cord plus 12-gauge appliance cords. Here is how to tell which one you need.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="which-cord" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Generator Cord Do You Need? Match the Largest Outlet on Your Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right generator cord is the one whose plug matches your generator's largest outlet and whose far end matches what you are plugging into. For most homes that means an L14-30 cord: a 4-prong, 30-amp, 125/250-volt twist-lock cord made of 10-gauge wire, running from the generator to an inlet box or manual transfer switch. Look at the generator's control panel before you shop, because the outlet decides everything.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">L14-30: The Standard Home Backup Cord</h3>

          <p>
            L14-30 is the 4-prong, 30-amp, 125/250-volt twist-lock outlet found on most portable generators from about 5,000 to 9,500 watts. It carries both 120-volt legs plus neutral and ground, so it can power 240-volt loads such as a well pump through a transfer switch or inlet box. It is the cord most of our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generators for power outages</a> are designed to use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">L5-30 and TT-30: 120-Volt, 30-Amp Cords</h3>

          <p>
            L5-30 is a 3-prong, 30-amp, 125-volt twist-lock outlet, and TT-30 is the 3-prong, 30-amp, 125-volt RV outlet found on many inverter generators. Both carry a single 120-volt leg, up to about 3,600 watts. They are right for RVs and 120-volt splitter cords, but they cannot feed a standard 240-volt L14-30 inlet box without an adapter, and even then only half the panel is powered.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">14-50: 50-Amp Cords for Large Generators</h3>

          <p>
            Generators of roughly 10,000 watts and up often add a 14-50R outlet, the 4-prong, 50-amp, 125/250-volt outlet. A 50-amp cord uses 6-gauge conductors and usually ends in an SS2-50R twist-lock connector to suit a 50-amp inlet box. Our guide to the <a href="/best-generator-with-50-amp-outlet/">best generators with a 50 amp outlet</a> lists the machines that have one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Household 5-15 and 5-20 Outlets</h3>

          <p>
            Every generator also has ordinary household outlets. These take normal extension cords, but the cord gauge must suit the appliance and the length. That is where the 12-gauge cord in our picks comes in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inlet-vs-splitter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Inlet Box Cord vs Splitter Cord: Two Different Jobs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator cord does one of two jobs. An inlet cord feeds the house wiring through an inlet box and a transfer switch or interlock kit, powering circuits at the panel. A splitter cord takes the same large generator outlet and breaks it out into household outlets at the far end, so you can plug appliances in directly. Decide which job you are doing before you buy, because the two cords are not interchangeable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inlet Box Cords: Female Connector at the Far End</h3>

          <p>
            An inlet cord has a male plug that goes into the generator and a female connector that goes onto the male inlet on your house. That orientation matters for safety: the exposed prongs are never live. Our <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">generator inlet box guide</a> covers the inlets, and <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> covers the part that keeps you from backfeeding the grid.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Splitter Cords: Household Outlets at the Far End</h3>

          <p>
            A splitter cord, sometimes called a spider or fan cord, has the same L14-30 or L5-30 plug at the generator end and a box of household outlets at the other. It lets one heavy cord cross the yard, with short cords from the box to each appliance. It is the tidy option for homes with no transfer switch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Use a Double-Male Cord</h3>

          <p>
            A cord with male plugs on both ends, sold online as a way to backfeed a house through a dryer or wall outlet, is dangerous. It leaves exposed live prongs and can send power back into the utility lines, endangering line workers. Use an inlet box with a transfer switch or interlock, installed to code, or run appliances on splitter and extension cords instead.
          </p>

