        <div id="gauge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. 10 Gauge vs 12 Gauge: The Gauge Follows the Amps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use 10-gauge cord for any 30-amp generator connection, 6-gauge for 50 amps, and at least 12-gauge for household cords carrying appliances. A lower gauge number means thicker copper, which runs cooler and loses less voltage. Buying a thinner cord than the circuit's rating is the most common and most dangerous generator cord mistake.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">10 Gauge for 30 Amps</h3>

          <p>
            L14-30, L5-30 and TT-30 cords should all be 10 gauge. Ten-gauge copper is what carries 30 amps safely, and every reputable 30-amp generator cord uses it. If a 30-amp cord's listing does not state 10 gauge, skip it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12 Gauge for Household Appliance Cords</h3>

          <p>
            For a cord from a generator's household outlet to a refrigerator, freezer or sump pump, 12 gauge is the sensible minimum at 25 to 50 feet. Many 12-gauge cords are rated 15 amps because of their plugs, which is plenty for one appliance. Thin 16-gauge cords are for lamps and chargers only.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">6 Gauge for 50 Amps</h3>

          <p>
            A 50-amp cord needs 6-gauge conductors, usually written 6/3+8/1 for three 6-gauge wires plus an 8-gauge ground. It is heavy and expensive, so only buy one if your generator has a 14-50R outlet and your inlet is 50 amps.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where to Get the Exact Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            This guide picks cords; the arithmetic of gauge, amps and distance lives in our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size guide</a>, which has the voltage-drop tables. Use it if your run is unusual or longer than 50 feet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Long Should a Generator Cord Be? 25 Feet Suits Most Homes
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most homes, a 25-foot generator cord is the right length: long enough to keep the generator at least 20 feet from the house, as the CDC recommends for carbon monoxide safety, and short enough to keep voltage drop small. Go to 50 feet only if the nearest safe spot is farther away, and to 10 feet only if the inlet is on a wall with no windows, doors or vents nearby.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Start From the Safe Generator Spot</h3>

          <p>
            Measure from where the generator can safely run, not from the inlet outward. The spot should be at least 20 feet from the house, downwind if possible, away from windows, doors and vents, and on firm, dry ground. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> walks through it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Then Measure the Path, Not the Straight Line</h3>

          <p>
            Run a tape along the route the cord will take, around the corner of the deck, along the foundation and up to the inlet. Add a few feet of slack so the cord is never stretched. The total is usually longer than people expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Longer Is Not Better</h3>

          <p>
            Every extra foot adds resistance and voltage drop, which makes motors such as refrigerator and well-pump compressors run warmer. A 10-gauge cord at 25 or 50 feet is comfortable for a 30-amp generator. Beyond that, check the numbers in the sizing guide before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="30-vs-50" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. 30 Amp vs 50 Amp Generator Cord: Buy for the Inlet, Not Just the Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a 30-amp L14-30 cord unless both your generator has a 14-50R outlet and your house has a 50-amp inlet. The cord can only carry what its weakest connection allows, so a 50-amp cord into a 30-amp inlet means an adapter and no extra power.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 30 Amps Delivers</h3>

          <p>
            A 30-amp, 240-volt connection carries up to about 7,200 watts, which covers the output of most portable generators up to about 9,500 starting watts. That runs a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, lights and a well pump on a typical transfer switch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 50 Amps Adds</h3>

          <p>
            A 50-amp connection carries up to about 12,000 watts, which suits generators of roughly 12,000 watts and up. If you own one, the 50-amp cord and inlet let you use all of it, including central air on some setups. Our <a href="/best-12000-watt-generator/">12,000 watt generator guide</a> covers the machines in that class.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching Generator, Cord and Inlet</h3>

          <p>
            Write down three things: the generator's largest outlet, the inlet type on the house, and the transfer switch or interlock rating. All three should agree. If you are still choosing, our guide on <a href="/how-to-hook-up-a-generator-to-your-house/">how to hook up a generator to your house</a> explains the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="jackets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. SJTW vs STW: Which Cord Jacket Holds Up Outdoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both SJTW and STW are outdoor-rated jackets, and either is fine for a home generator cord. STW is the heavier of the two, with thicker insulation rated for 600 volts, while SJTW is junior service rated for 300 volts. STW is worth having on a long cord that lives outside or gets dragged across rough ground.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Letters</h3>

          <p>
            S means service grade, J means junior service with a lighter jacket, T means thermoplastic and W means rated for outdoor use. A cord with no W is not for outdoors. Every pick in this guide carries the W.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold-Weather Flexibility</h3>

          <p>
            Vinyl jackets stiffen in the cold, which matters when you are uncoiling a cord in an ice storm. Many listings state a temperature range such as -40°F to 140°F; prefer cords that publish one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Listing Marks</h3>

          <p>
            Look for UL, ETL or cETL listing on the cord. These marks mean an independent lab has certified the cord to the relevant safety standard, which is the best protection against undersized copper in a no-name cord.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rain-snow" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Run a Generator Cord Safely in Rain and Snow
          </h2>

          <p>
            Keep the connections dry, not just the cord. An outdoor-rated jacket handles rain, but the plug and connector are the weak points. Keep the twist-lock connection off the ground and covered, use the inlet box's weatherproof cover, and never let a connection sit in a puddle or snowbank.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Generator End</h3>

          <p>
            Run the generator under a purpose-made running cover or tent, which also shelters the plug. Our <a href="/best-generator-cover/">generator cover guide</a> and the explainer on <a href="/can-you-run-a-generator-in-the-rain/">running a generator in the rain</a> cover the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Protect the Far End</h3>

          <p>
            For an inlet box, the box's flip cover shields the connection. For a splitter cord, set the outlet box on a block or bucket and cover it with a weatherproof connection box. Do not route cords through a door or window that has to stay partly open, because that lets exhaust in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep the Path Clear</h3>

          <p>
            Route the cord where people will not trip over it or drive over it. Never run cords under rugs, and never bury them in snow, where a shovel can cut them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adapters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Generator Cord Adapters: When They Are Fine and When to Skip Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            Short adapter cords, often called dogbones, are fine for matching connector types at the same or lower amp rating, such as using a 50-amp outlet with a 30-amp cord. They are not fine for stepping up, such as feeding a 30-amp inlet from a 20-amp outlet, or for turning a 120-volt outlet into a 240-volt one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Safe Adapters</h3>

          <p>
            Adapters from a larger outlet to a smaller cord, such as 14-50R to L14-30, are common and useful if you own a large generator but a 30-amp inlet. The catch is that the generator's 50-amp breaker no longer protects a 30-amp cord, so the generator breaker in your transfer switch or interlock must be rated 30 amps, and you must keep the load within it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adapters to Avoid</h3>

          <p>
            Avoid adapters that connect a 120-volt TT-30 or L5-30 outlet to a 240-volt L14-30 inlet unless your transfer switch maker approves it. At best they power only one leg of the panel. Avoid any adapter without a UL or ETL mark.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Right Cord Instead</h3>

          <p>
            If you find yourself stacking two adapters, buy the correct cord. Every connection adds resistance and another place for water to get in. For the generator itself, our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">portable generator guide</a> notes each model's outlets. Indoors, plug sensitive electronics into a <a href="/best-surge-protector/">surge protector</a> rather than straight into a splitter cord.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Cord Before Checking the Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An L14-30, L5-30 and TT-30 look similar in a product photo but do not fit each other. Read the label next to your generator's largest outlet and the inlet on the house, then buy the cord that matches both. Guessing leads to adapters, returns or a cord that only powers half the panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Thin Cord for a Big Load</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 14 or 16-gauge household cord on a refrigerator or sump pump at 50 feet starves the motor of voltage and heats up. Use 10 gauge for any 30-amp generator cord and at least 12 gauge for appliance cords, and check the sizing guide for long runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Short Cord and Running the Generator Close</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 10-foot cord is tempting because it is cheap and easy to handle, but it pulls the generator toward the house. Carbon monoxide from a generator near a window, door or vent is the most serious risk of outage power. Buy the cord that reaches a spot at least 20 feet away.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding With a Double-Male Cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cord with plugs on both ends, used to push power into a wall or dryer outlet, leaves live prongs exposed and can energize the utility lines. It is illegal in most places and has killed line workers. Install an inlet box with a transfer switch or interlock instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Connection in a Puddle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Outdoor cords tolerate rain, but connections do not like sitting in water. Keep the twist-lock joint and any splitter box off the ground and covered, use the inlet box's weatherproof lid, and route cords where they will not end up under snow or standing water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best generator cord?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the best generator cord is the Champion Power Equipment 25-Foot 30-Amp L14-30 cord. It uses 10-gauge wire, matches the L14-30 outlet on most portable generators and the L14-30 inlet box on most homes, and is long enough to keep the generator a safe distance from the house. Larger generators with a 14-50 outlet need a 50-amp cord instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What gauge cord do I need for a 30 amp generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30-amp generator cord should be 10 gauge. Ten-gauge copper carries 30 amps safely at typical generator cord lengths of 25 to 50 feet, which is why reputable L14-30, L5-30 and TT-30 cords all use it. A 12-gauge cord is too thin for a 30-amp connection. For long runs, check voltage drop in a sizing guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a generator cord be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most homes need 25 feet, and 50 feet is common where the safe generator spot is farther away. Longer cords work but add voltage drop, which makes motors run warmer, so a 100-foot run may need a heavier gauge. Always choose the length that keeps the generator at least 20 feet from the house rather than the shortest cord available.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between L14-30 and TT-30?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                L14-30 is a 4-prong, 30-amp, 125/250-volt twist-lock connection that carries both legs of power and suits home inlet boxes and transfer switches. TT-30 is a 3-prong, 30-amp, 125-volt RV connection that carries one leg. A TT-30 cord suits RVs and 120-volt splitters but cannot properly feed a 240-volt house inlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 50 amp generator cord?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your generator has a 14-50R outlet and your house has a 50-amp inlet box. A 50-amp cord carries up to about 12,000 watts and suits generators of roughly 12,000 watts and up. If your generator's largest outlet is an L14-30, a 30-amp cord is correct, and a 50-amp cord adds nothing but weight and cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a regular extension cord with a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for running individual appliances from the generator's household outlets, as long as the cord is outdoor rated and heavy enough. Use at least 12 gauge for a refrigerator, freezer or sump pump at 25 to 50 feet. Regular cords cannot connect a generator to house wiring; that needs a twist-lock cord, an inlet box and a transfer switch or interlock.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to plug a generator into a wall outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Plugging a generator into a wall or dryer outlet with a double-male cord backfeeds the house wiring, leaves live prongs exposed and can send power into utility lines, endangering line workers. It is illegal in most places. The safe way to power house circuits is an inlet box with a transfer switch or interlock kit, installed to code.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

